WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
In the '90s, Sinbad was a near-constant presence
on TV and at the movies.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.620
But we don't hear too much from this one-named
comedian anymore.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.880
So just where did Sinbad go?

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.080
Let's find out!

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.920
One of the main reasons Sinbad isn't as prolific
as he used to be is because his health isn't

00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:17.500
always one hundred percent.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.720
In 2010, for example, he had to undergo knee
replacement surgery, which is a costly and

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.820
painful procedure that requires a lot of recovery
time.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.360
Patients essentially have to re-learn to walk.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.740
Sinbad received two artificial joints, or
"bionic knees," as he calls them.

00:00:32.749 --> 00:00:37.199
Alas, the new knees left one of his legs slightly
longer than the other, which made his gait

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.500
become what he refers to as "a pimp walk."

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.680
"And what leg's shorter than the other, 'cause
I had my knees replaced.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.520
So one leg just didn't bend all the way out.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:44.780
It gives me a pimp walk.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.300
Some people think I'm just pimp walking."

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.600
Sinbad was knocked out of commission again
five years later, when his chronic back pain

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:56.649
grew so unbearable that in 2015, on his 59th
birthday, no less, he had to undergo double

00:00:56.649 --> 00:00:58.079
fusion spinal surgery.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.120
The procedure lasted for 10 hours.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.240
But as of 2019, he's still alive and kicking.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.820
Sinbad was huge in the '90s.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.080
He was on TV all the time throughout the decade,
partly because he churned out comedy special

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.960
after comedy special that ran on the likes
of HBO and the fledgling Comedy Central.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:20.400
They aired less and less as Comedy Central
developed a larger slate of original programming,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.520
and perhaps as a result, Sinbad produced fewer
specials.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.440
After 1998's Nothin' But the Funk, he focused
on movies and sitcoms and didn't tape another

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.500
solo show until 2010's appropriately titled
Where U Been?

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.500
Four years later, he delivered Make Me Wanna
Holla, but it's been pretty slow going since

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:37.500
then.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:43.160
Sinbad fans may remember Mark Curry, one of
his '90s standup comedy contemporaries and

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.020
the star of the ABC sitcom Hangin' With Mr.
Cooper.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:51.140
In 2006, Curry was doing his laundry at home
when a can of spray starch fell behind a hot

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:56.160
water heater and exploded, leaving second-degree
burns over 20 percent of his body.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:01.159
Even after a three-day medically-induced coma,
the pain was so bad that Curry contemplated

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:02.159
suicide.

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:06.889
In response, Curry's comedian friends, particularly
Sinbad, jumped into action.

00:02:06.889 --> 00:02:10.679
They talked with Curry on the phone and were
generally just there for him in an effort

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.060
to lift his spirits.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.459
Curry later spoke with People Magazine about
his journey out of the ordeal, revealing that

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:20.200
Sinbad making him laugh was one of the things
that lifted his spirits.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:25.120
Sinbad is still a touring comic, but if he's
gracing a stage these days, it's far more

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.300
likely that he's doing it as a musician.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.500
After buying a drum kit and learning to play
a couple years back, he formed a band for

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.760
the first time in more than 30 years.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.740
It's a jazz-funk group called Memphis Red
and the Stank Nasty Band.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:42.160
Sinbad fronts the group, singing and playing
guitar under the stage name of his alter ego

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.160
"Memphis Red."

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:48.680
Musical performances as Memphis Red happened
to bookend Sinbad's 2014 comedy special Make

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:49.760
Me Wanna Holla.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.820
Hopefully his funk starts influencing his
comedy and vice versa, if it hasn't already.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:00.780
In December 2009, the IRS went after Sinbad
for millions in uncollected taxes.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:04.080
He apparently hadn't paid federal income tax
in about a decade.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.110
It was so bad that a judge ordered that he
sell his $1.5 million house in Hidden Hills,

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:14.500
California to help alleviate some of the $8.15
million he owed the government.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:19.220
To protect himself, he filed for Chapter 7
bankruptcy, but the case was later dismissed

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.340
because he filled out the wrong paperwork.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:26.260
Regardless, Sinbad was still on the hook for
his crushing tax debt, and he ended up filing

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.840
for bankruptcy again in 2013.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:33.380
Around that time, he explained that his financial
problems were not a result of squandering.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.300
"My thing is, I didn't buy Bentleys.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.340
I didn't live large.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:37.580
I believed in me.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:40.110
I invested in me, and I invested in a lot
of other people.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.489
I would not change it.

00:03:41.489 --> 00:03:42.480
I would not go back."

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.420
A year after filing for bankruptcy for the
first time, Sinbad resorted to something that

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:50.960
a lot of formerly prominent stars have done:
reality TV.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.660
In 2010, he appeared on The Celebrity Apprentice,
albeit very briefly, getting fired on the

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:01.920
second episode of the season amidst a disagreement
with bandana enthusiast Bret Michaels.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.440
Sinbad later joked during an appearance on
Oprah: Where Are They Now? about his regret

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:07.760
from being on the show.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.200
"Who the heck is Donald Trump to fire me?

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.960
I regret I didn't tell Donald Trump, 'You
need to fire your barber.'"

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.840
In 2011, Sinbad starred in his own reality
show, entitled Sinbad: It's Just Family.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.900
It depicted his unique home life, as he and
his wife, Meredith, divorced in 1992 after

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.000
six years of marriage but then remarried in
2002.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.500
Alas, the series was canceled after just one
season.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:37.300
Sinbad got back into scripted TV in 2018 on
the FOX sitcom Rel, in which he played Lil

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:41.880
Rel Howery's dad, but that also ended up being
cancelled after only one season.

